    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "**BUILD SUCCESS** \u2014 all 66 tests pass, including the new `Cp54Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag so it returns `true` when an owner's city already holds 40\u201349 owners (approaching the hard limit of 50), while leaving the hard rejection at 50 unchanged.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Computed the per-city count once (`ownersInCity`), kept the existing `>= 50` \u2192 `409 CONFLICT` rejection, and set `capacityWarning = ownersInCity >= 40 && ownersInCity <= 49` on the new owner. Because the count excludes the owner being created, a fresh owner joining a city that already holds 40\u201349 gets the warning.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the persisted `capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with getter/setter, mirroring `bulkSignupWarning`.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the owner schema, so MapStruct maps it onto `OwnerDto` by name (same as the other derived warning flags).\n\n4. **Schema files** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column to the `owners` table.\n\nThe design follows the existing conventions for the sibling `bulkSignupWarning` / `possibleDuplicate` derived flags: computed at create time, persisted, and surfaced on read.",
